{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 29.\tx360\tab CLAIMS\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Claims represent analytical conclusions and must be separate from objective facts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Claim types may include: Strength Weakness Opportunity Threat Risk Assessment Competitive Position Franchise Quality Growth Driver Margin Driver ROE Driver Valuation Driver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Required claim fields: claim_id claim_type statement subject_entity_id supporting_entity_ids confidence evidence_strength rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Confidence: High Medium Low\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Evidence strength: Direct Strong Inference Moderate Inference Weak Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 30.\tx360\tab EVIDENCE AND PROVENANCE\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Evidence should support factual entities, relationships, metrics and claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Recommended evidence fields: evidence_id entity_or_claim_id evidence_type source_name source_reference description confidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Recommended provenance pattern: Entity/Relationship/Claim \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? SUPPORTED_BY \u8594 ? Evidence Evidence \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? FROM \u8594 ? Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 31.\tx360\tab NODE VS PROPERTY RULE\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Create an entity when the concept: \u8226 ? Can be shared across companies \u8226 ? Has its own relationships \u8226 ? Has analytical importance \u8226 ? Can be compared \u8226 ? Can participate in causal relationships\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Use properties for descriptive values such as: legal_name ticker isin website description value unit period confidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note (v2): the same rule applies one level down, to relationships. A relationship that needs its own data (an ownership percentage, an exposure amount, a rating outlook) is promoted to a relationship entity with properties (e.g. OwnershipStake, ExposureRelation, CreditRating) rather than left as a bare directional edge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 32.\tx360\tab ENTITY NORMALIZATION\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Entity ID format: _\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples: company_hdfc_bank product_home_loan risk_credit_risk metric_roe customer_retail segment_private_sector_bank person_ceo_hdfc rating_agency_crisil macro_repo_rate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rules: \u8226 ? Lowercase \u8226 ? Underscores \u8226 ? No punctuation \u8226 ? Stable naming \u8226 ? No random IDs \u8226 ? Reuse universal concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 33.\tx360\tab EVENT EXCLUSION\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Strictly exclude: Event News Announcement Timeline EarningsEvent MarketEvent AcquisitionEvent ManagementChangeEvent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Capture persistent state instead of the event that created it. Note (v2): this applies to governance too \u8212- a GovernanceRole captures a person's standing role (e.g. currently serving as CEO), not the appointment event that created it. A rating change is likewise captured as the current CreditRating state, not a RatingActionEvent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 34.\tx360\tab HIGH-LEVEL GRAPH MODEL\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Banking \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_COMPANY \u8594 ? Company\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Company \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? BELONGS_TO_SEGMENT \u8594 ? BankingSegment \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? PART_OF_GROUP \u8594 ? CorporateGroup \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? OFFERS_PRODUCT \u8594 ? Product \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? PROVIDES_SERVICE \u8594 ? Service \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? SERVES \u8594 ? CustomerSegment \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? FUNDED_BY \u8594 ? FundingSource \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_ASSET_EXPOSURE_TO \u8594 ? AssetClass \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_LOAN_EXPOSURE_TO \u8594 ? LoanSegment \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_REVENUE_STREAM \u8594 ? RevenueStream \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? USES_CHANNEL \u8594 ? DistributionChannel \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_CAPABILITY \u8594 ? Capability \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? USES_TECHNOLOGY \u8594 ? Technology \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? COMPETES_WITH \u8594 ? Company \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? COMPETES_IN \u8594 ? CompetitiveArena \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_ADVANTAGE \u8594 ? CompetitiveAdvantage \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_STRENGTH \u8594 ? Strength \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_WEAKNESS \u8594 ? Weakness \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_OPPORTUNITY \u8594 ? Opportunity \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? EXPOSED_TO \u8594 ? Risk \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? DEPENDS_ON \u8594 ? Dependency \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_DRIVER \u8594 ? Driver \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? REGULATED_BY \u8594 ? Regulator \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_LICENSE \u8594 ? License \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? PARTNERS_WITH \u8594 ? Partnership \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? PART_OF_ECOSYSTEM \u8594 ? Ecosystem \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? POSITIONED_AS \u8594 ? StrategicPosition \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_VALUATION_DRIVER \u8594 ? Driver \u9500 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? RATED_BY \u8594 ? CreditRatingAgency (v2) \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HAS_RATING \u8594 ? CreditRating (v2)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additional (v2): Person \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HOLDS_ROLE \u8594 ? Company; Person \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? MEMBER_OF \u8594 ? PromoterGroup; Shareholder / PromoterGroup \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? HELD_BY \u8594 ? Company; MacroeconomicFactor \u9492 ?\u9472 ?\u9472 ? INFLUENCES \u8594 ? Driver / Company\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 35.\tx360\tab ONTOLOGY OBJECTIVE\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ontology is designed to answer: \u8226 ? What is the company? \u8226 ? How does it make money? \u8226 ? Who are its customers? \u8226 ? What products and services does it offer? \u8226 ? How is it funded? \u8226 ? Where are its assets deployed? \u8226 ? What capabilities does it possess? \u8226 ? What creates competitive advantage? \u8226 ? What are its structural risks? \u8226 ? What drives growth? \u8226 ? What drives profitability and ROE? \u8226 ? What influences franchise quality and valuation? \u8226 ? How does it compare with competitors? \u8226 ? (v2) Who governs and owns the company? \u8226 ? (v2) How do rating agencies assess it? \u8226 ? (v2) What external macroeconomic factors influence its performance?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The graph represents persistent, structured, analyst-oriented company knowledge. It is intentionally event-free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 36.\tx360\tab GOVERNANCE AND OWNERSHIP (v2 EXTENSION)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Entities: Person PromoterGroup Shareholder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Person properties: name role_category (Executive, Board Member, or Promoter)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PromoterGroup properties: name description\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shareholder properties: name holder_type (Promoter, FII, DII, Mutual Fund, Insurance, or Retail)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Relationships: HOLDS_ROLE (Person \u8594 ? Company, carries title and role_category \u8212- a persistent standing role, not an appointment event) MEMBER_OF (Person \u8594 ? PromoterGroup) HELD_BY (Shareholder or PromoterGroup \u8594 ? Company, carries a percentage and reporting period)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rationale: without these entities the ontology can describe what a company does but not who runs or controls it \u8212- promoter shareholding, board composition, and management tenure are standing structural facts, not events, and belong in Layer 1 alongside CorporateGroup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 37.\tx360\tab CREDIT RATINGS (v2 EXTENSION)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Entities: CreditRatingAgency CreditRating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 CreditRatingAgency properties: name (e.g. CRISIL, ICRA, Moody's, S&P)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 CreditRating properties: scale_value outlook (Stable, Positive, Negative, or Under Review) rated_entity_type (Issuer rating or Instrument rating) period\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Relationships: RATED_BY (Company \u8594 ? CreditRatingAgency) HAS_RATING (Company \u8594 ? CreditRating; CreditRatingAgency \u8594 ? CreditRating)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rationale: CreditRating is modeled as its own entity, not a property on RATED_BY, because a company typically carries multiple concurrent ratings (issuer rating, instrument-specific ratings, ratings from different agencies) \u8212- each needs its own scale, outlook, and period, consistent with how MetricObservation already separates a metric definition from its company-specific value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 38.\tx360\tab MACROECONOMIC FACTORS (v2 EXTENSION)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Entity: MacroeconomicFactor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Properties: name (e.g. Repo Rate, GDP Growth, Inflation, Credit Cycle Phase, Systemic Liquidity) category (Monetary Policy, Growth, Inflation, Liquidity, or Credit Cycle)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Relationships: uses the existing causal vocabulary from section 23 (INFLUENCES, INCREASES, REDUCES, EXPOSES_TO) rather than introducing new relationship types.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rationale: the causal-chain examples in section 23 (CASA Ratio \u8594 ? Cost of Funds \u8594 ? NIM \u8594 ? ROA) are entirely internal to the company. Real driver chains for banks routinely originate outside the company \u8212- policy rate moves, systemic liquidity, the credit cycle \u8212- and prior to this entity such external drivers had no node to attach to.\par}
